/-!
# The abelian structure and the derived category of simplicial modules

Let `C` be a site and let `A_•` be a simplicial sheaf of rings on `C`. Stacks 09D1 defines a
simplicial `A_•`-module to be a *sheaf of modules over the sheaf of rings on `Δ × C` associated
to `A_•`*, and `Stacks.SimplicialModules.SimplicialModule` renders this as the full subcategory
of presheaves of modules over `Stacks.SimplicialModules.associatedRingPresheaf` whose underlying
abelian presheaf is a sheaf for the topology `Δ × C` inherits from `C` along the second
projection. This file supplies the two things that rendering left open and that `D(A_•)` needs:

* that the associated presheaf of rings really is a sheaf for the inherited topology, so that
  the category is Mathlib's `SheafOfModules` over an honest sheaf of rings — this is the half of
  Stacks 09D1 that speaks of "the sheaf of rings on `Δ × C`";
* that the resulting category is abelian and admits a derived category, which is what the
  untagged prose preceding Stacks 09D2 presupposes when it writes `D(A_•)`.

The sheaf condition is proved from a general statement about the site `Δ × C`: a presheaf on a
product `D × C` is a sheaf for the topology inherited from `(C, J)` along `CategoryTheory.Prod.snd`
as soon as each of its restrictions to a fixed first coordinate is a `J`-sheaf. This is what makes
the levelwise sheaf condition on `A_•` enough.

## Main definitions

* `CategoryTheory.Functor.prodSectionSnd` and `CategoryTheory.Functor.prodSectionFst`: the two
  coordinate sections `U ↦ (d, U)` and `d ↦ (d, U)` of a product category.
* `Stacks.SimplicialModules.ringSheaf`: the presheaf of rings on `Δ × C` associated to `A_•`,
  read as a sheaf of rings for the inherited topology.
* `Stacks.SimplicialModules.sheafOfModulesEquivalence`: a category of sheaves of modules written
  as a full subcategory of presheaves of modules is Mathlib's `SheafOfModules`.
* `Stacks.SimplicialModules.equivSheafOfModules`: the instance of the previous equivalence at
  `SimplicialModule J A`.
* `Stacks.SimplicialModules.abelian`: the abelian structure on `SimplicialModule J A`.
* `Stacks.SimplicialModules.hasDerivedCategory`: the standard derived category of
  `SimplicialModule J A`, that is `D(A_•)`.
* `Stacks.SimplicialModules.DerivedSimplicialModule`: `D(A_•)` itself.

## Main results

* `CategoryTheory.Sieve.generate_eq_functorPullback_generate_map`: if every arrow of a presieve is
  strongly `p`-cartesian, the sieve it generates is the `p`-preimage of the sieve generated by the
  images. This is what identifies the covering sieves of an inherited topology.
* `CategoryTheory.Presieve.isSheaf_inheritedTopology`: a presheaf of types on a fibred category is
  a sheaf for the inherited topology of Stacks 06NV as soon as it is a sheaf for every preimage of
  a covering sieve of the base.
* `CategoryTheory.Presheaf.isSheaf_inheritedTopology_prodSnd`: on `D × C` with the topology
  inherited along the second projection, being a sheaf is a coordinatewise condition.
* `Stacks.SimplicialModules.isSheaf_associatedRingPresheaf`: the associated presheaf of rings is a
  sheaf, which is the content of `ringSheaf`.

## Implementation notes

The reduction in `CategoryTheory.Presieve.isSheafFor_functorPullback_snd` is the one-line argument
that every arrow `(c, d) : (d₁, V) ⟶ (d₀, U)` of `D × C` factors as `(c, 𝟙) ≫ (𝟙, d)`, so that a
compatible family on the preimage of a sieve `S` on `U` is determined by its values on the arrows
`(𝟙, d)` with `d` in `S`, where it is exactly a compatible family for `S` on the restriction of
the presheaf to the slice `{d₀} × C`. No pullbacks in `C` and no smallness are used, which is why
the statement applies to `Δ × C` for an arbitrary site `C`.

The module universe is `max u v r w` with `w` free, matching
`Stacks.Cohomology.SiteModules.moduleSheavesAbelian`: sheafification of abelian presheaves and the
generators used there are available in that universe, which is what supplies the abelian structure.

`abelian` and `hasDerivedCategory` are plain declarations installed with
`attribute [local instance]` rather than global instances. The hom universe of the derived
category is a free parameter of `HasDerivedCategory`, and a global instance fixing it to the
standard choice would fire with metavariable universes throughout the library; this is the same
treatment `Stacks.Cohomology.SiteModules.site_isGrothendieckAbelian_addCommGrp` gets.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 09D1](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09D1)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 06NV](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/06NV), the inherited site.
* [Stacks Project, Tag 09D2](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09D2), whose statement is what
  `D(A_•)` is needed for.
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits Opposite

universe w v u r v₁ u₁ v₂ u₂ v₃ u₃ v₄ u₄ w₁

namespace CategoryTheory

section InheritedTopology

variable {𝒮 : Type u₁} [Category.{v₁} 𝒮] {𝒳 : Type u₂} [Category.{v₂} 𝒳]

/-- If every arrow of a presieve `R` on `X` is strongly `p`-cartesian, then the sieve generated by
`R` is the `p`-preimage of the sieve generated by the family of images of `R`: the inclusion from
left to right is functoriality, and the one from right to left is the universal property of a
strongly cartesian arrow applied to the factorisation of `p g` through an image. -/
lemma Sieve.generate_eq_functorPullback_generate_map (p : 𝒳 ⥤ 𝒮) {X : 𝒳} (R : Presieve X)
    (hR : ∀ ⦃Y : 𝒳⦄ (g : Y ⟶ X), R g → p.IsStronglyCartesian (p.map g) g) :
    Sieve.generate R = Sieve.functorPullback p (Sieve.generate (R.map p)) := by
  ext Y g
  constructor
  · rintro ⟨Z, h, f, hf, rfl⟩
    exact ⟨p.obj Z, p.map h, p.map f, ⟨hf⟩, by simp⟩
  · rintro ⟨Z', h, f', hf', hg⟩
    cases hf' with
    | @of Z f hf =>
      have := hR f hf
      exact ⟨Z, Functor.IsStronglyCartesian.map p (p.map f) f hg.symm g, f, hf,
        Functor.IsStronglyCartesian.fac p (p.map f) f hg.symm g⟩

/-- A presheaf of types on a fibred category `p : 𝒳 ⥤ 𝒮` is a sheaf for the topology `𝒳` inherits
from `(𝒮, J)` (Stacks 06NV) as soon as it is a sheaf for the `p`-preimage of every covering sieve
of the base. The covering families of the inherited topology consist of strongly cartesian arrows,
so the sieves they generate are exactly such preimages. -/
theorem Presieve.isSheaf_inheritedTopology (p : 𝒳 ⥤ 𝒮) (J : GrothendieckTopology 𝒮)
    (Q : 𝒳ᵒᵖ ⥤ Type w₁)
    (h : ∀ (X : 𝒳) (S : Sieve (p.obj X)), S ∈ J (p.obj X) →
      Presieve.IsSheafFor Q (Sieve.functorPullback p S).arrows) :
    Presieve.IsSheaf (p.inheritedTopology J) Q := by
  rw [Functor.inheritedTopology_eq, Precoverage.isSheaf_toGrothendieck_iff]
  intro X Y f R hR
  rw [Sieve.generate_eq_functorPullback_generate_map p R hR.1,
    ← Sieve.functorPullback_pullback]
  exact h Y _ (J.pullback_stable _ hR.2)

end InheritedTopology

section ProductSite

variable {D : Type u₃} [Category.{v₃} D] {C : Type u₄} [Category.{v₄} C]

/-- The section `U ↦ (d, U)` of the second projection of a product category. -/
@[simps]
def Functor.prodSectionSnd (d : D) : C ⥤ D × C where
  obj U := (d, U)
  map f := (𝟙 d, f)

/-- The section `d ↦ (d, U)` of the first projection of a product category. -/
@[simps]
def Functor.prodSectionFst (U : C) : D ⥤ D × C where
  obj d := (d, U)
  map f := (f, 𝟙 U)

/-- A presheaf of types on `D × C` is a sheaf for the preimage of a sieve `S` on `U` under the
second projection as soon as its restriction to the slice `{d} × C` is a sheaf for `S`.

A compatible family on the preimage of `S` is determined by its values on the arrows `(𝟙 d, b)`
with `b` in `S`, because every arrow `g` of `D × C` into `(d, U)` factors as
`(g.1, 𝟙) ≫ (𝟙 d, g.2)`; on those arrows it is exactly a compatible family for `S` on the
restricted presheaf. -/
theorem Presieve.isSheafFor_functorPullback_snd (Q : (D × C)ᵒᵖ ⥤ Type w₁) (X : D × C)
    (S : Sieve ((Prod.snd D C).obj X))
    (hS : Presieve.IsSheafFor ((Functor.prodSectionSnd X.1).op ⋙ Q) S.arrows) :
    Presieve.IsSheafFor Q (Sieve.functorPullback (Prod.snd D C) S).arrows := by
  intro x hxc
  have hx := hxc.to_sieveCompatible
  have key : ∀ {Y : D × C} {f f' : Y ⟶ X} (_ : f = f')
      (hf : (Sieve.functorPullback (Prod.snd D C) S).arrows f)
      (hf' : (Sieve.functorPullback (Prod.snd D C) S).arrows f'), x f hf = x f' hf' := by
    intro Y f f' hff' hf hf'
    subst hff'
    rfl
  have hyc : ∀ y : Presieve.FamilyOfElements ((Functor.prodSectionSnd X.1).op ⋙ Q) S.arrows,
      (∀ {V : C} (b : V ⟶ (Prod.snd D C).obj X) (hb : S.arrows b),
        y b hb = x ((Functor.prodSectionSnd X.1).map b) hb) → y.Compatible := by
    intro y hy
    rw [Presieve.compatible_iff_sieveCompatible]
    intro V W b c hb
    rw [hy, hy]
    exact (key ((Functor.prodSectionSnd X.1).map_comp c b) _ _).trans
      (hx ((Functor.prodSectionSnd X.1).map b) ((Functor.prodSectionSnd X.1).map c) hb)
  obtain ⟨t, ht, ht'⟩ := hS _ (hyc _ fun _ _ => rfl)
  refine ⟨t, fun Y g hg => ?_, fun t' ht2 => ht' t' fun _ b hb =>
    ht2 ((Functor.prodSectionSnd X.1).map b) hb⟩
  have hfac : (Functor.prodSectionFst Y.2).map g.1 ≫ (Functor.prodSectionSnd X.1).map g.2 = g :=
    Prod.ext (Category.comp_id g.1) (Category.id_comp g.2)
  have hm : x ((Functor.prodSectionSnd X.1).map g.2) hg
      = Q.map ((Functor.prodSectionSnd X.1).map g.2).op t := (ht g.2 hg).symm
  have h3 := hx ((Functor.prodSectionSnd X.1).map g.2)
    ((Functor.prodSectionFst Y.2).map g.1) hg
  rw [hm] at h3
  have h5 : ∀ u : Y ⟶ X,
      u = (Functor.prodSectionFst Y.2).map g.1 ≫ (Functor.prodSectionSnd X.1).map g.2 →
      Q.map (Quiver.Hom.op u) t
        = Q.map (Quiver.Hom.op ((Functor.prodSectionFst Y.2).map g.1))
            (Q.map (Quiver.Hom.op ((Functor.prodSectionSnd X.1).map g.2)) t) := by
    rintro u rfl
    exact Functor.map_comp_apply Q (Quiver.Hom.op ((Functor.prodSectionSnd X.1).map g.2))
      (Quiver.Hom.op ((Functor.prodSectionFst Y.2).map g.1)) t
  rw [h5 g hfac.symm]
  exact h3.symm.trans (key hfac _ hg)

variable {A : Type u₁} [Category.{v₁} A]

/-- On `D × C` equipped with the topology inherited from `(C, J)` along the second projection,
being a sheaf is a coordinatewise condition: a presheaf is a sheaf as soon as each of its
restrictions to a slice `{d} × C` is a `J`-sheaf. -/
theorem Presheaf.isSheaf_inheritedTopology_prodSnd (J : GrothendieckTopology C)
    (P : (D × C)ᵒᵖ ⥤ A)
    (h : ∀ d : D, Presheaf.IsSheaf J ((Functor.prodSectionSnd d).op ⋙ P)) :
    Presheaf.IsSheaf ((Prod.snd D C).inheritedTopology J) P := by
  intro E
  exact Presieve.isSheaf_inheritedTopology _ _ _ fun X S hS =>
    Presieve.isSheafFor_functorPullback_snd _ X S (h X.1 E S hS)

end ProductSite

end CategoryTheory

namespace Stacks.SimplicialModules

section Bundling

variable {C : Type u} [Category.{v} C] {K : GrothendieckTopology C} (S : Sheaf K RingCat.{r})

/-- A sheaf of modules written as a presheaf of modules together with the sheaf condition, read
as an object of Mathlib's `SheafOfModules`. -/
@[simps]
def toSheafOfModules' : ObjectProperty.FullSubcategory
    (fun M : PresheafOfModules.{w} S.obj ↦ Presheaf.IsSheaf K M.presheaf) ⥤
      SheafOfModules.{w} S where
  obj M := ⟨M.obj, M.property⟩
  map f := ⟨f.hom⟩

/-- An object of Mathlib's `SheafOfModules`, read as a presheaf of modules together with the
sheaf condition. -/
@[simps]
def ofSheafOfModules' : SheafOfModules.{w} S ⥤ ObjectProperty.FullSubcategory
    (fun M : PresheafOfModules.{w} S.obj ↦ Presheaf.IsSheaf K M.presheaf) where
  obj M := ⟨M.val, M.isSheaf⟩
  map f := ObjectProperty.homMk f.val

/-- The two bundlings of the category of sheaves of modules over a sheaf of rings — as a full
subcategory of presheaves of modules, and as Mathlib's `SheafOfModules` — agree. Both functors
are the identity on the underlying data, so the unit and counit are the identity. -/
def sheafOfModulesEquivalence : ObjectProperty.FullSubcategory
    (fun M : PresheafOfModules.{w} S.obj ↦ Presheaf.IsSheaf K M.presheaf) ≌
      SheafOfModules.{w} S where
  functor := toSheafOfModules' S
  inverse := ofSheafOfModules' S
  unitIso := Iso.refl _
  counitIso := Iso.refl _
  functor_unitIso_comp _ := rfl

end Bundling

section

variable {C : Type u} [Category.{v} C] (J : GrothendieckTopology C)
  (A : SimplicialObject (Sheaf J RingCat.{r}))

/-- The presheaf of rings on `Δ × C` obtained by uncurrying a simplicial sheaf of rings is a sheaf
for the topology inherited from `C` along the second projection.

Being a sheaf on `Δ × C` is a coordinatewise condition, by
`CategoryTheory.Presheaf.isSheaf_inheritedTopology_prodSnd`, and in the coordinate `n` the
presheaf is the underlying presheaf of the sheaf of rings `A_n`. -/
theorem isSheaf_associatedRingPresheaf :
    Presheaf.IsSheaf ((CategoryTheory.Prod.snd SimplexCategory C).inheritedTopology J)
      (associatedRingPresheaf J A) := by
  refine Presheaf.isSheaf_inheritedTopology_prodSnd J _ fun n => ?_
  exact (Presheaf.isSheaf_of_iso_iff
    (NatIso.ofComponents (fun _ => Iso.refl _) (by intros; simp [Functor.prodSectionSnd]) :
      (Functor.prodSectionSnd n).op ⋙ associatedRingPresheaf J A ≅
        (A.obj (op n)).obj)).2 (A.obj (op n)).property

/-- **Stacks 09D1.** The sheaf of rings on `Δ × C` associated to a simplicial sheaf of rings.
The sheaf condition is `isSheaf_associatedRingPresheaf`. -/
@[stacks 09D1 "the sheaf of rings on Δ × C associated to A_•"]
def ringSheaf :
    Sheaf ((CategoryTheory.Prod.snd SimplexCategory C).inheritedTopology J) RingCat.{r} :=
  ⟨associatedRingPresheaf J A, isSheaf_associatedRingPresheaf J A⟩

/-- Simplicial `A_•`-modules are the sheaves of modules over the sheaf of rings on `Δ × C`
associated to `A_•`, in Mathlib's bundling. -/
def equivSheafOfModules :
    SimplicialModule.{w, v, u, r} J A ≌ SheafOfModules.{w} (ringSheaf J A) :=
  sheafOfModulesEquivalence (ringSheaf J A)

/-- Simplicial modules have finite products: they are the sheaves of modules of
`equivSheafOfModules`. -/
theorem hasFiniteProducts :
    HasFiniteProducts (SimplicialModule.{max u v r w, v, u, r} J A) :=
  have := Stacks.Cohomology.SiteModules.moduleSheavesAbelian.{u, v, r, w} (ringSheaf J A)
  ⟨fun _ => Adjunction.hasLimitsOfShape_of_equivalence
    (equivSheafOfModules.{max u v r w, v, u, r} J A).functor⟩

/-- **The category of simplicial modules is abelian.** This is what makes `D(A_•)` exist, and it
is presupposed by the untagged prose preceding Stacks 09D2.

The category is the category of sheaves of modules over the sheaf of rings `ringSheaf J A` on
`Δ × C`, by `equivSheafOfModules`, and that category is abelian by
`Stacks.Cohomology.SiteModules.moduleSheavesAbelian`. -/
@[instance_reducible, sga "4 IV 11.1.2" "special case"]
def abelian : Abelian (SimplicialModule.{max u v r w, v, u, r} J A) :=
  have := Stacks.Cohomology.SiteModules.moduleSheavesAbelian.{u, v, r, w} (ringSheaf J A)
  have := hasFiniteProducts.{w} J A
  abelianOfEquivalence (equivSheafOfModules.{max u v r w, v, u, r} J A).functor

attribute [local instance] abelian

/-- **The derived category `D(A_•)` of simplicial modules exists.** The standard choice of
localisation of cochain complexes of simplicial modules at the quasi-isomorphisms. -/
@[instance_reducible]
def hasDerivedCategory :
    HasDerivedCategory.{max (r + 1) (u + 1) (v + 1) (w + 1)}
      (SimplicialModule.{max u v r w, v, u, r} J A) :=
  HasDerivedCategory.standard _

attribute [local instance] hasDerivedCategory

/-- `D(A_•)`, the derived category of the category of simplicial `A_•`-modules. -/
abbrev DerivedSimplicialModule :=
  DerivedCategory (SimplicialModule.{max u v r w, v, u, r} J A)

end

end Stacks.SimplicialModules

end
